    q: "What are the surgical options for renal cell carcinoma (RCC), and when is immunotherapy used for metastatic disease?",
    a: "<b>Renal cell carcinoma — management:</b><br><b>Localized RCC (surgical):</b><ul><li><b>Partial nephrectomy</b> (nephron-sparing) — preferred for tumors &lt;7 cm (T1), solitary kidney, bilateral tumors, CKD; equivalent cancer-specific survival to radical nephrectomy for T1</li><li><b>Radical nephrectomy</b> — for large tumors (&gt;7 cm), central tumors not amenable to partial, or renal vein/IVC tumor thrombus</li></ul><b>Ablative techniques:</b><ul><li>Radiofrequency ablation (RFA) or cryoablation: for small (&lt;3 cm) tumors in patients with high surgical risk or solitary kidney</li></ul><b>Active surveillance:</b> Small renal masses (&lt;2 cm) in elderly or comorbid patients<br><b>Metastatic RCC (systemic therapy):</b><ul><li>Favorable risk: <b>sunitinib or pazopanib (TKIs)</b> as first-line, OR <b>nivolumab + ipilimumab</b> (CheckMate 214)</li><li>Poor/intermediate risk: <b>Nivolumab + ipilimumab</b> (PD-1 + CTLA-4 blockade) — first-line (superior OS)</li><li>Subsequent lines: cabozantinib, lenvatinib/everolimus, belzutifan (VHL pathway inhibitor — hereditary RCC)</li></ul>"
