    q: "Describe active surveillance vs. radical prostatectomy vs. radiation therapy for localized prostate cancer (low, intermediate, and high risk).",
    a: "<b>Prostate cancer — treatment by risk group:</b><br><b>Low risk (Gleason 6, PSA &lt;10, T1-T2a):</b><ul><li><b>Active surveillance (AS)</b> — preferred for most; PSA q6 months, DRE annually, repeat biopsy at 1 year then q2–3 years or MRI-guided</li><li>Definitive treatment (surgery or radiation) if reclassification to higher risk or patient preference</li></ul><b>Intermediate risk (Gleason 7 or PSA 10–20 or T2b-T2c):</b><ul><li>Favorable intermediate: AS appropriate in select patients</li><li>Unfavorable intermediate: definitive treatment warranted</li><li>Options: <b>Radical prostatectomy (RP)</b> or <b>radiation (EBRT + brachytherapy ± ADT × 4–6 months)</b> — equivalent cancer-specific survival</li></ul><b>High risk (Gleason ≥8 or PSA &gt;20 or T3-T4):</b><ul><li><b>Radiation (EBRT + brachytherapy) + ADT × 18–36 months</b> OR <b>radical prostatectomy + pelvic LN dissection</b></li><li>ADT: LHRH agonist (leuprolide) or antagonist (degarelix)</li></ul><b>Metastatic prostate cancer:</b> ADT + abiraterone/enzalutamide/docetaxel (combination superior to ADT alone)"
