    a: "<b>Ectopic pregnancy — medical vs. surgical management:</b><br><b>Medical management with methotrexate (MTX) — criteria for eligibility:</b><ul><li>Hemodynamically <b>stable</b></li><li><b>Unruptured</b> ectopic pregnancy</li><li>Ectopic mass &lt;<b>3.5 cm</b></li><li><b>No fetal cardiac activity</b> on ultrasound</li><li>hCG: most guidelines use &lt;5000–10,000 mIU/mL (lower hCG = higher success)</li><li>No contraindications to MTX (renal/hepatic disease, immunocompromised, breastfeeding, blood dyscrasias, peptic ulcer, pulmonary disease)</li></ul><b>Dosing:</b> Single-dose MTX 50 mg/m² IM; check hCG days 4 and 7 — expect 15% decrease between days 4→7<br><b>Surgical management (salpingostomy or salpingectomy):</b><ul><li>Hemodynamic instability, suspected rupture (peritoneal signs, hemoperitoneum)</li><li>Contraindication to MTX</li><li>Failed MTX (hCG not declining or rising)</li><li>Large ectopic (&gt;3.5 cm) or cardiac activity present</li></ul>"
