    q: "What is the surgical staging and management of endometrial cancer?",
    a: "<b>Endometrial cancer — surgical staging and management:</b><br><b>Most common gynecologic malignancy in the US (uterine endometrioid adenocarcinoma most common type)</b><br><b>Risk factors:</b> Unopposed estrogen exposure, obesity, tamoxifen use, Lynch syndrome, nulliparity, late menopause<br><b>Standard surgical staging (FIGO):</b><ul><li><b>Total hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (TH + BSO)</b></li><li><b>Pelvic lymph node dissection</b> ± para-aortic lymph node sampling</li><li>Peritoneal washings</li><li>Omentectomy if serous/clear cell histology</li></ul><b>Staging determines adjuvant treatment:</b><ul><li>Stage I, Grade 1–2, limited myometrial invasion: surgery alone</li><li>Stage I, Grade 3 or deep invasion: vaginal brachytherapy ± external beam radiation</li><li>Stage III–IV or high-grade histology: chemotherapy (carboplatin + paclitaxel) ± radiation</li></ul><b>Conservative management (fertility-sparing):</b><ul><li>Young patients with Stage IA, Grade 1, no myometrial invasion: progestins (medroxyprogesterone 200–400 mg/day) — must have D&amp;C confirmation; surgery after childbearing complete</li></ul>"
