    q: "What is the surgical management of colorectal cancer by stage?",
    a: "<b>Colorectal cancer (CRC) — surgical management by stage:</b><br><b>Stage I (T1-2N0M0):</b><ul><li>Surgical resection alone — colectomy with margins + lymphadenectomy (minimum 12 lymph nodes examined)</li><li>T1 polyp cancer: polypectomy may be sufficient if margins clear, no lymphovascular invasion, well-differentiated</li></ul><b>Stage II (T3-4N0M0):</b><ul><li>Surgical resection; adjuvant chemotherapy controversial (benefit in high-risk Stage II: T4, perforation, inadequate node sampling, BRAF mutant MSS tumors)</li><li>MSI-H/dMMR tumors: better prognosis; may not benefit from 5-FU-based chemo</li></ul><b>Stage III (any T, N+, M0):</b><ul><li>Surgical resection + adjuvant chemotherapy (<b>FOLFOX or CAPOX × 3–6 months</b>)</li></ul><b>Stage IV (M1):</b><ul><li>If resectable mets (liver/lung): <b>curative resection</b> of primary + mets (simultaneous or staged); ~25% 5-year survival</li><li>If unresectable: systemic chemotherapy ± targeted therapy (bevacizumab, cetuximab/panitumumab for RAS WT); palliative intent</li></ul><b>Rectal cancer:</b><ul><li>Stage II–III: neoadjuvant chemoradiation (5-FU + radiation) → surgery → adjuvant FOLFOX</li><li>Total mesorectal excision (TME) — essential for adequate surgical margins</li></ul>"
