    q: "What is the management of gallstone pancreatitis, and when should cholecystectomy be performed?",
    a: "<b>Gallstone (biliary) pancreatitis management:</b><br><b>Acute management:</b><ul><li>IV fluid resuscitation (aggressive: lactated Ringer's preferred over NS — reduces SIRS)</li><li>NPO → advance diet as tolerated (early oral/enteral feeding improves outcomes)</li><li>Analgesia (IV opioids)</li><li>No antibiotics unless infected necrosis/cholangitis suspected</li></ul><b>ERCP indications in gallstone pancreatitis:</b><ul><li>Concurrent <b>acute cholangitis</b> → ERCP within 24h (emergency)</li><li>Persistent biliary obstruction — ERCP within 24–72h</li><li>No indications: mild-moderate pancreatitis with no cholangitis/obstruction (ERCP does not improve outcomes)</li></ul><b>Timing of cholecystectomy:</b><ul><li><b>Mild pancreatitis:</b> Laparoscopic cholecystectomy during <b>same admission</b> (index hospitalization) — significantly reduces risk of recurrence (pancreatitis recurrence ~25–30% without cholecystectomy)</li><li><b>Severe/necrotizing pancreatitis:</b> Delay cholecystectomy until recovery (6–8 weeks) and all collections resolved</li></ul><b>Severity assessment:</b><ul><li>BISAP score, Ranson criteria, or APACHE II at 48h</li><li>CT severity index (CTSI) at 48–72h if clinically severe</li></ul>"
