    a: "<b>Splenic trauma — non-operative management (NOM) criteria:</b><br><b>AAST Splenic Injury Grading:</b><ul><li>Grade I: subcapsular hematoma &lt;10%, minor laceration &lt;1 cm deep</li><li>Grade II: hematoma 10–50%, laceration 1–3 cm</li><li>Grade III: hematoma &gt;50% or expanding, laceration &gt;3 cm into hilum</li><li>Grade IV: laceration involving segmental vessels, hilar injury</li><li>Grade V: shattered spleen or hilar devascularization</li></ul><b>NOM indicated if ALL:</b><ul><li><b>Hemodynamically stable</b> (SBP ≥90, HR ≤100 without ongoing transfusion)</li><li>No peritonitis</li><li>No other abdominal injuries requiring laparotomy</li><li>Grade I–III (Grade IV/V with stable hemodynamics may also be managed with angioembolization)</li></ul><b>Angioembolization (adjunct to NOM):</b><ul><li>Active extravasation on CT angiography in stable patient</li><li>Pseudoaneurysm</li><li>Grade III–V with active bleeding</li></ul><b>Emergent splenectomy required if:</b><ul><li>Hemodynamic instability despite resuscitation</li><li>Failed angioembolization</li><li>Peritonitis</li><li>Associated hollow viscus injury requiring laparotomy</li></ul>"
