    a: "<b>Asymptomatic gallstones — watchful waiting vs. cholecystectomy:</b><br><b>General principle:</b> <b>Asymptomatic gallstones do NOT require prophylactic cholecystectomy</b> in most patients<br><b>Annual risk of developing symptoms:</b> ~1–4%; of developing complications (cholecystitis, pancreatitis): 0.1–0.3%/year<br><b>Exceptions — cholecystectomy recommended for asymptomatic gallstones in:</b><ul><li><b>Porcelain gallbladder</b> (calcified gallbladder wall) — controversial; risk of gallbladder cancer historically cited but newer data shows selective calcification patterns carry higher risk than diffuse</li><li><b>Gallbladder polyps ≥10 mm</b> (risk of malignancy)</li><li>Large stones (&gt;3 cm) — possibly higher risk of gallbladder cancer</li><li>Patients undergoing bariatric surgery (rapid weight loss increases gallstone formation and complications)</li><li>Transplant candidates (before immunosuppression)</li><li>Hemolytic anemia with pigmented stones (planned splenectomy — concurrent cholecystectomy)</li><li>Sickle cell disease (prevents future complications in immunocompromised state)</li></ul>"
